<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meinhard II of Gorizia-Tyrol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1238–<a href="/years/1295">1295</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Meinhard, Duke of Carinthia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meinhard II was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> who, in the family division of 1271, took Tyrol and left Gorizia to his brother Albert. He built a unified territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Merano</a> to the Inn valley with a strong administration and its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage. He supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> and was rewarded with the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> in 1286. His daughter Elisabeth married Albert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meinhard-ii-of-gorizia-tyrol.jpg" alt="Meinhard II of Gorizia-Tyrol" width="263" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meinhard_II.,_Graf_von_Tirol_und_G%C3%B6rz,_Herzog_von_K%C3%A4rnten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
